====================================================================================================
CRITTER MEMORY LAB  --  What are the biggest impacts to memory usage?
====================================================================================================
  Host          : Raspberry Pi 5 Model B Rev 1.1  (aarch64, 4 CPUs, 8,062 MB RAM)
  OS / kernel   : Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie)  /  6.18.50+rpt-rpi-2712
  Page size     : 16,384 bytes  (RSS is counted in whole pages)
  Compiler      : gcc (Debian 14.2.0-19) 14.2.0
  C library     : glibc 2.41
  Measurement   : /usr/bin/time -v  (time (GNU Time) UNKNOWN)
  CPU governor  : performance
  ASLR          : 2  (setarch -R: True)
  Repeats       : 10 per configuration (median reported)
  Data volume   : small N=20,000 (code-dominated), large N=1,000,000 (data-dominated)
  Process floor : 1,088 KB max RSS for an empty main() at -O2
  Logs          : /home/jaj154/ECE2160_HW4/logs/memlab_20261102_154830
  WARNING   : ASLR is on (normal). It adds small run-to-run RSS jitter; use --no-aslr to run every
             binary under 'setarch -R'.

  Running 144 tests ...

####################################################################################################
# SUITE: FLAGS  --  Compiler & linker flags, one at a time  (113 tests)
####################################################################################################
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[F-IO-01] -O0  [io]    (suite: flags)
  TEST      : Build the I/O unit (high-rate acquisition) at -O2 and again with '-O0'; run each 10x at
             N=20,000 under time -v; read image sizes with size(1) and the stack high-water mark
             from a probe build.
  TESTING   : Does '-O0' change resident memory, image size or stack depth?
  HYPOTHESIS: INCREASES
  BASELINE  : -O2 (baseline)               -O2
  VARIANT   : -O0                          -O0
  RESULTS   : metric                        baseline       variant   delta                   verdict
             max RSS (time -v)             1,584 KB      1,584 KB   0 KB (+0.0%)            NEUTRAL (+/-32 KB)
             image text+data+bss            20.7 KB       23.8 KB   +3,188 B (+15.0%)       INCREASES
             tracked heap peak             784.0 KB      784.0 KB   0 B (+0.0%)             NEUTRAL
             stack high-water               2,888 B       3,048 B   +160 B (+5.5%)          INCREASES
             file on disk (info)            71.7 KB       72.0 KB
             minor faults (info)                136           136
             run time (info)                0.000 s       0.000 s
  OUTCOME   : INCREASES (stack only)   hypothesis INCREASES -> CONFIRMED   output: identical
  MEANING   : '-O0' raises stack high-water by 160 B (5.5%) versus '-O2 (baseline)'. Resident memory
             on Linux did not move measurably because demand paging only charges pages that are
             touched and RSS is counted in whole pages. No register allocation: every local lives in
             a stack slot, nothing is inlined and tail calls are not eliminated, so frames and code
             both grow. Measured at N=20,000 (code/runtime-dominated), this bounds what the lever
             can ever save; the process floor alone is 1,088 KB. On an MMU-less microcontroller the
             image change (+3,188 B (+15.0%)) is paid in full in flash/RAM, which Linux demand
             paging hides.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
