[F-LARGE-01] -O0 at production volume [mem]    (suite: flags)
  TEST      : Same comparison as the small-N flag tests, but the memory unit processes N=1,000,000
             samples (data-dominated regime).
  TESTING   : Once data dominate the footprint, does '-O0' still matter?
  HYPOTHESIS: NEUTRAL
  BASELINE  : -O2 (baseline)               -O2
  VARIANT   : -O0                          -O0
  RESULTS   : metric                        baseline       variant   delta                   verdict
             max RSS (time -v)              31.5 MB       31.5 MB   0 KB (+0.0%)            NEUTRAL (+/-162 KB)
             image text+data+bss            99.8 KB      103.8 KB   +4,016 B (+3.9%)        INCREASES
             tracked heap peak             30.57 MB      30.57 MB   0 B (+0.0%)             NEUTRAL
             file on disk (info)            71.9 KB       72.3 KB
             minor faults (info)              2,547         2,547
             run time (info)                0.320 s       0.520 s
  OUTCOME   : INCREASES (image only)   hypothesis NEUTRAL -> REFUTED   output: identical
  MEANING   : '-O0' raises image text+data+bss by 4,016 B (3.9%) versus '-O2 (baseline)'. Resident
             memory on Linux did not move measurably because demand paging only charges pages that
             are touched and RSS is counted in whole pages. Compiler flags act on code (tens of KB);
             at this N the heap holds tens of MB, so even a real code-size change is a rounding
             error in resident memory. At N=1,000,000 the baseline needs 31.5 MB resident, so the
             change is 0.0% of what the Critter must provision. On an MMU-less microcontroller the
             image change (+4,016 B (+3.9%)) is paid in full in flash/RAM, which Linux demand paging
             hides. Run time changed +62% (0.320 s -> 0.520 s): a memory/time trade-off to weigh,
             not ignore. Hypothesis (NEUTRAL) refuted: worth a closer look in the report (check the
             image breakdown in builds.log and the phase snapshots in phases.csv).
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
