####################################################################################################
# SUITE: ALLOC  --  Allocation strategy (heap / static / bounded) and allocator policy  (4 tests)
####################################################################################################
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[A-01] Growable vector -> exact heap allocation [io]    (suite: alloc)
  TEST      : Run the I/O unit with --strategy=heap (one malloc of N records) instead of realloc
             doubling.
  TESTING   : What does not knowing the size in advance cost?
  HYPOTHESIS: REDUCES
  BASELINE  : grow                         -O2
  VARIANT   : heap                         -O2  --strategy=heap
  RESULTS   : metric                        baseline       variant   delta                   verdict
             max RSS (time -v)              24.0 MB       24.0 MB   0 KB (+0.0%)            NEUTRAL (+/-123 KB)
             image text+data+bss            20.7 KB       20.7 KB   0 B (+0.0%)             NEUTRAL
             tracked heap peak             24.02 MB      22.89 MB   -1.12 MB (-4.7%)        REDUCES
             file on disk (info)            71.7 KB       71.7 KB
             minor faults (info)              1,570         1,565
             run time (info)                0.030 s       0.030 s
  OUTCOME   : NEUTRAL   hypothesis REDUCES -> REFUTED   output: identical
  MEANING   : No memory dimension moved beyond measurement noise (RSS threshold +/-123 KB). Doubling
             rounds capacity up to the next power of two and realloc may briefly hold old and new
             blocks. Compare tracked heap (bytes reserved) with max RSS (pages touched): on Linux
             the unused tail of a large mmap'd block is never touched, and glibc grows big blocks
             with mremap instead of copying, so the waste is address space, not RAM. On an MCU heap
             the same tail is real, unusable RAM. At N=1,000,000 the baseline needs 24.0 MB
             resident, so the change is 0.0% of what the Critter must provision. Hypothesis
             (REDUCES) refuted: worth a closer look in the report (check the image breakdown in
             builds.log and the phase snapshots in phases.csv).
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[A-02] Growable vector -> static .bss buffer [io]    (suite: alloc)
  TEST      : Rebuild with -DCRITTER_IO_STATIC_CAP=1000000 and run --strategy=static.
  TESTING   : Heap vs. statically reserved buffer: resident cost and image cost.
  HYPOTHESIS: REDUCES
  BASELINE  : grow                         -O2
  VARIANT   : static                       -O2  -DCRITTER_IO_STATIC_CAP=1000000  --strategy=static
  RESULTS   : metric                        baseline       variant   delta                   verdict
             max RSS (time -v)              24.0 MB       24.0 MB   0 KB (+0.0%)            NEUTRAL (+/-123 KB)
             image text+data+bss            20.7 KB      22.91 MB   +22.89 MB (+113154.7%)  INCREASES
             tracked heap peak             24.02 MB           0 B   -24.02 MB (-100.0%)     REDUCES
             file on disk (info)            71.7 KB       71.7 KB
             minor faults (info)              1,570         1,564
             run time (info)                0.030 s       0.030 s
  OUTCOME   : INCREASES (image only)   hypothesis REDUCES -> REFUTED   output: identical
  MEANING   : 'static' raises image text+data+bss by 22.89 MB (113154.7%) versus 'grow'. Resident
             memory on Linux did not move measurably because demand paging only charges pages that
             are touched and RSS is counted in whole pages. Resident cost equals the exact-heap case
             (pages are touched the same way) but the buffer moves into .bss: the image grows by N x
             record size. On an MCU that is RAM reserved at link time, which is exactly what makes
             worst-case usage provable. At N=1,000,000 the baseline needs 24.0 MB resident, so the
             change is 0.0% of what the Critter must provision. On an MMU-less microcontroller the
             image change (+22.89 MB (+113154.7%)) is paid in full in flash/RAM, which Linux demand
             paging hides. Trade-off: tracked heap peak REDUCES (-24.02 MB (-100.0%)). Hypothesis
             (REDUCES) refuted: worth a closer look in the report (check the image breakdown in
             builds.log and the phase snapshots in phases.csv).
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[A-03] Store-everything -> bounded ring buffer [io]    (suite: alloc)
  TEST      : Run the I/O unit with --strategy=ring: keep only the current W=1000-record window.
  TESTING   : Does the I/O unit need to retain the raw stream at all?
  HYPOTHESIS: REDUCES
  BASELINE  : grow                         -O2
  VARIANT   : ring                         -O2  --strategy=ring
  RESULTS   : metric                        baseline       variant   delta                   verdict
             max RSS (time -v)              24.0 MB      1,088 KB   -23.0 MB (-95.6%)       REDUCES (+/-123 KB)
             image text+data+bss            20.7 KB       20.7 KB   0 B (+0.0%)             NEUTRAL
             tracked heap peak             24.02 MB       23.4 KB   -23.99 MB (-99.9%)      REDUCES
             file on disk (info)            71.7 KB       71.7 KB
             minor faults (info)              1,570           102
             run time (info)                0.030 s       0.030 s
  OUTCOME   : REDUCES (resident)   hypothesis REDUCES -> CONFIRMED   output: identical
  MEANING   : 'ring' lowers max RSS (time -v) by 23.0 MB (95.6%) versus 'grow'. Summaries are
             computed as each window fills, so memory is O(W) instead of O(N) and the output is bit-
             identical. This is the single most important embedded pattern: bounded buffers make
             memory independent of uptime. At N=1,000,000 the baseline needs 24.0 MB resident, so
             the change is 95.6% of what the Critter must provision.
