             source passes or fails depending on the optimisation flags: stack budgets must be
             proven per build configuration.

####################################################################################################
# SUITE: SCALING  --  Memory vs data volume  (4 tests)
####################################################################################################
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[C-01] I/O unit: store-everything vs ring buffer    (suite: scaling)
  TEST      : Run each configuration at N = 125,000, 250,000, 500,000, 1,000,000 and fit maxRSS =
             floor + slope x N.
  TESTING   : How does memory grow with the amount of data (i.e. with uptime between uploads)?
  RESULTS   : max RSS by N
             configuration              125,000     250,000     500,000   1,000,000
             grow                      4,144 KB    7,216 KB     12.5 MB     24.0 MB
             ring                      1,088 KB    1,088 KB    1,088 KB    1,088 KB
  OUTCOME   :
             grow                  O(N): 23.9 B/sample; a 64 MB budget holds ~2,754,622 samples (~7.7 h at 100 Hz)
             ring                  BOUNDED (memory independent of N)
  MEANING   : 'grow' costs 23.9 bytes per sample retained, 'ring' 0.0 (effectively flat). The slope
             (bytes per sample) is the design's true memory cost. A slope near zero means memory is
             bounded and the device can run indefinitely; any positive slope is a maximum uptime
             before it runs out of RAM.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
